# Answer 4 · The Global Political Economy and Pakistani Women (CSS 2018)
@py question 2018-6

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define the global political economy, then trace its impacts on Pakistani women channel by channel — positive and negative — and end with policy.
@ask What are the IMPACTS of the GLOBAL POLITICAL ECONOMY on PAKISTANI WOMEN?
(1) Introduction: the global political economy is the interplay of states, markets and institutions; its impacts on Pakistani women are mixed
(2) Pakistan's place: a peripheral exporter of textiles and labour, dependent on external finance
(3) Trade and textiles: jobs in garment units; low wages; home-based piece work
(4) Global supply chains and standards: Ali Enterprises fire; Atlanta Agreement; GSP+ conditions
(5) Debt and IMF programmes: price rises, sales tax and spending pressure add to women's burdens
(6) Migration and remittances: men abroad, women heading households; record remittances
(7) Agriculture and world prices: cotton pickers and food security
(8) Technology and the digital economy: freelancing, mobile money, a 25% gap
(9) Global norms: CEDAW, Beijing, SDG 5 and donor programmes shaping laws
(10) Culture and backlash: media, consumerism, conservative reaction
(11) Policy and conclusion: protect workers, value care, close the digital gap
:::

## Decoding the question
The **"global political economy"** means the interplay of **states, markets and international institutions** — trade, finance, debt, migration, multinational companies, and bodies such as the IMF, WTO and UN. **"What are the impacts"** asks for a list of effects, organised and evidenced. Organise by **channel**, give **positive and negative** impacts under each, with Pakistani data, and end with **policy**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
The **global political economy** is the system through which **states, markets and international institutions** — the IMF, World Bank, WTO, UN and multinational companies — shape how wealth and power are produced and shared across the world.

#### ii) The argument
Over the last two decades it has affected Pakistani women through **trade, supply chains, debt, migration, agriculture, technology, global norms and culture**. The impacts are **mixed**: some new income, rights and information, but also insecure work, heavier unpaid burdens and backlash. Women gain where **labour rights and social protection** are strong, and lose where they are weak — as they mostly are in Pakistan's informal economy.

### 2. Pakistan's place in the world economy
In the language of world-system theory, Pakistan sits in the **periphery or lower semi-periphery** of the world economy (in **Wallerstein**'s terms). It exports **textiles, cotton and rice**, exports **labour** to the Gulf, imports fuel and machinery, and depends on **external finance** from the IMF and other lenders. Women are concentrated at the **bottom** of these chains — as home-based stitchers, cotton pickers and unpaid family helpers — where earnings are lowest and protection weakest. Pakistan's ranking of ==144th of 145== on **economic participation** in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026 shows how little of the global economy's gains have reached them.

### 3. Trade and textiles

#### i) Jobs
Textiles and garments are Pakistan's largest export sector. Garment units in Karachi, Lahore and Faisalabad employ women in stitching, finishing and packing. For some urban women these are the first formal jobs in their families, bringing income and a measure of independence.

#### ii) Low wages and home-based work
Much of the work is **subcontracted** to **home-based workers** who stitch, embroider and pack on **piece rates**, without contracts or social security. **Maria Mies**'s term "**housewifisation**" fits: calling them housewives lets employers pay them little. **Sindh** recognised them in the **Home-Based Workers Act 2018**.

### 4. Global supply chains and standards

#### i) Safety
The fire at **Ali Enterprises** in Karachi (==11 September 2012==) killed about 260 workers in a factory producing for a European brand, exposing weak safety in global chains.

#### ii) Unintended effects
After the **Atlanta Agreement** (==1997==) on child labour, Sialkot's football stitching moved from homes into stitching centres; many **women homeworkers** who could not travel lost income.

#### iii) Trade conditions
The EU's **GSP+** (from ==2014==) gives Pakistan duty-free access on condition that it implements 27 conventions, including **CEDAW** and core labour standards — giving activists leverage.

### 5. Debt and IMF programmes

#### i) Conditions
Pakistan has had repeated **IMF programmes** since ==1988==, most recently a **7-billion-dollar Extended Fund Facility** (==2024==). Conditions include higher **electricity and gas prices**, wider **sales tax** and tighter spending.

#### ii) Gendered effects
Women manage household budgets, so **price rises** hit them first; pressure on **health and education** budgets increases their unpaid care. Indirect taxes such as sales tax fall heavily on poor households, and cuts to development spending delay schools, clinics and water schemes that save women's time. **Diane Elson** warned that such policies treat women's unpaid labour as "infinitely elastic".

#### iii) Protection
The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), which pays cash to women, has been **expanded** as a safety net during IMF programmes.

### 6. Migration and remittances

#### i) Men abroad
Millions of Pakistani men work in the Gulf, Europe and elsewhere, drawn by global demand for construction and service labour. Remittances reached a record of more than **38 billion dollars** in 2024–25.

#### ii) Women at home
Many women manage households, finances and children alone. This can increase their **autonomy** and decision-making, but also their **workload** and isolation, and they often remain under the control of in-laws. Some women themselves migrate as domestic workers or nurses, facing the risks of global care chains described by **Arlie Hochschild** (==2000==).

### 7. Agriculture and world prices
Most employed women work in **agriculture**. **Cotton-picking** women earn very little for hard, seasonal work whose price is linked to **world cotton markets**. Global food-price shocks (as in ==2022==) hit women responsible for feeding families. Climate shocks linked to global emissions — the ==2022== floods — destroyed women's livestock and home-based work, showing another channel through which the world economy reaches Pakistani women.

### 8. Technology and the digital economy

#### i) Opportunities
Online **freelancing**, e-commerce, mobile money and digital learning offer home-based income to women whose mobility is restricted.

#### ii) Limits
Platform work is insecure and unprotected. The **mobile-internet gender gap** is still ==25%== (GSMA, 2024–25); many women's phone use is controlled by family members, and online harassment deters them.

### 9. Global norms and institutions
The **Beijing** conference (==1995==), **CEDAW** (acceded ==1996==) and **SDG 5** have shaped Pakistan's laws and institutions: the **NCSW** (==2000==), **60** reserved seats (==2002==), and laws on harassment (==2010==), honour killing (==2016==) and child marriage (==2025==). Donor programmes funded research and NGOs — sometimes criticised as **donor-driven**. Still, these global commitments give Pakistani women's groups a standard against which to hold their own government to account.

### 10. Culture and backlash
Global media and consumer culture spread new images of women's lives — and **beauty industries** selling skin-lightening creams. Rapid change also provokes **conservative backlash**, with calls to protect "our culture" from foreign influence, often through tighter control of women.

::: fig mapping | The global political economy and its impacts on Pakistani women.
@left Global channel
@right Main impact on women
Textile exports => Low-paid and home-based piece work
Supply-chain standards => Safety scrutiny; GSP+ leverage
IMF programmes => Price rises; more unpaid care; BISP expanded
Migration and remittances => Women heading households
World commodity prices => Cotton pickers' low wages
Digital economy => Home-based income; 25% gap
Global norms => New laws and institutions
:::

### 11. Policy and conclusion

#### i) Policy
Extend **labour protection** and social security to home-based, domestic and garment workers, as Sindh's 2018 Act begins to do; enforce **factory safety** and building codes; **gender-assess** IMF programmes and protect BISP, health and education; build **safe transport and childcare**; close the **digital gap**; and use **GSP+** reviews to push rights and labour standards.

#### ii) Conclusion
Over the last two decades, the global political economy has given some Pakistani women **new work, money and rights language**, but it has also placed them at the **bottom of global chains** and added to their **unpaid burdens**. With female labour-force participation at ==24.4%==, Pakistan has captured less of globalisation's benefits for women than its neighbours. The impacts are not fixed: **national policy** decides whether women gain or lose.

## Why this answer scores
- Defines the **global political economy** and organises impacts by **channel**.
- Every channel has **Pakistani evidence** — Ali Enterprises, Atlanta Agreement, GSP+, IMF 2024, remittances, the 25% digital gap.
- Balances **positive and negative** impacts and ends with **policy**.
- A **mapping figure** summarises channels and impacts.
